on concurrent Updates
Suppose an accounts table has id = 1 with balance = 100. Two transactions (Tx A and Tx B) start at the same time to subtract 20 from balance.
1. Tx A executes: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 20 WHERE id = 1; (Acquires row-level lock on id = 1, creates new tuple version 80, does not commit yet).
2. Tx B executes: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 20 WHERE id = 1; (Blocked by Tx A's row lock; Tx B waits).
3. Tx A executes: COMMIT; (Releases lock; version 80 is now committed).
What happens to Tx B next depends on the isolation level:
• Read Committed: Tx B wakes up, fetches the newly committed row version (80), re-evaluates its WHERE clause, and updates it to 60. Result: Both updates succeed (balance = 60). (Note: If Tx B had read 100 in an earlier SELECT and ran SET balance = 80, it would cause a Lost Update).
• Repeatable Read: Tx B wakes up and sees that row id = 1 was modified after Tx B's transaction snapshot began. Because PostgreSQL cannot merge changes into an older snapshot, Tx B immediately fails with ERROR: could not serialize access due to concurrent update.
• Serializable (SSI): Behaves identically to Repeatable Read here. Tx B aborts with ERROR: could not serialize access due to concurrent update and must be retried by your application.

