PostgreSQL divides all table and index files into fixed-size 8KB blocks (pages) and uses HOT pruning to clean up dead row versions on individual pages without waiting for a full VACUUM. When tables still become fragmented or bloated over time, pg_repack rebuilds
and compacts them online without blocking application reads and writes.
PostgreSQL does not store a table as one continuous stream of bytes; it splits every table file on disk into an array of fixed 8KB (8,192-byte) pages (also called blocks). Every ctid (block_number, tuple_offset) points directly to one of these 8KB pages.
