kafka EOS
Kafka EOS is achieved through four layers. First, the producer enables idempotency (enable.idempotence=true),
which uses a Producer ID (PID) and sequence numbers so broker retries do not create duplicate records. Second,
Kafka transactions (transactional.id) allow multiple writes across topics, partitions, and even offset commits to be committed atomically,
ensuring either all records are visible or none are. Third, brokers maintain transaction metadata, fence old producers using producer epochs,
and hide uncommitted or aborted records. Fourth, consumers use isolation.level=read_committed so they only read committed transactional data.

Together, these provide exactly-once guarantees for Kafka → Process → Kafka workflows, including atomic offset commits via sendOffsetsToTransaction(). However, Kafka EOS does not guarantee exactly-once business operations involving external systems such as PostgreSQL, payment gateways, or REST APIs. For those,
application-level safeguards such as idempotency keys, unique constraints, processed_event tables, or deduplication logic are still required. tables, or deduplication logic are still required.

